Q: What&#39;s the simplest way to reverse the order of bits in a byte in C/C++? A: <p>On modern architecture, bit-twiddling hacks are pessimal. They get in the way of the compiler figuring out what you’re trying to do and generating efficient SIMD code for it.</p>
<p>The following C++20 implementation, compiled literally without optimizations, would copy each byte of input to a bitfield, copy the bits to a second bitfield one by one, then copy that intermediate result to the output. (Before C++20, in order to avoid undefined behavior, it would be necessary to call <code>memcpy()</code> rather than <code>std::bit_Cast</code>, which would prevent the function from being <code>constexpr</code>.) In reality, since that’s straightforward enough for the compiler to figure out what you’re doing, many mainstream compilers can optimize it.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;bit&gt;
#include &lt;cstdint&gt;

using std::uint8_t;

struct Bitfield8 {
    uint8_t b0 : 1;
    uint8_t b1 : 1;
    uint8_t b2 : 1;
    uint8_t b3 : 1;
    uint8_t b4 : 1;
    uint8_t b5 : 1;
    uint8_t b6 : 1;
    uint8_t b7 : 1;
};

constexpr uint8_t revByte(const uint8_t in) noexcept
{
    const Bitfield8 inBits = std::bit_cast&lt;Bitfield8&gt;(in);
    const Bitfield8 outBits = {
        .b0 = inBits.b7,
        .b1 = inBits.b6,
        .b2 = inBits.b5,
        .b3 = inBits.b4,
        .b4 = inBits.b3,
        .b5 = inBits.b2,
        .b6 = inBits.b1,
        .b7 = inBits.b0
    };
    return std::bit_cast&lt;uint8_t&gt;(outBits);
}
</code></pre>
<p>With a bare-bones test driver</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt; // transform
#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;
#include &lt;iterator&gt;  // begin, cbegin, cend

alignas(16) extern uint8_t data[1024];

int main()
{
    std::transform(std::cbegin(data), std::cend(data), std::begin(data), revByte);
    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Clang 21.1.0 with <code>-O3 -march=armv9.6-a -fno-unroll-loops</code> will recognize what’s going on here and optimize to a single <code>rbit</code> vector instruction:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>.LBB0_1:
        ldr     q0, [x9, x8]
        rbit    v0.16b, v0.16b
        str     q0, [x9, x8]
        add     x8, x8, #16
        cbne    x8, x10, .LBB0_1
</code></pre>
<p>And without <code>-fno-unroll-loops</code>, it unrolls and inlines the entire loop into a series of interleaved  <code>ldp</code>, <code>rbit</code>, <code>stp</code> instructions.</p>
<p>ICX 2026 or Clang 22.1 with <code>-O3 -march=x86-64-v4 -fno-unroll-loops</code> auto-vectorizes this to:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>.LBB0_1:
        vmovdqu ymm3, ymmword ptr [rcx + rax]
        vpand   ymm4, ymm3, ymm0
        vpshufb ymm4, ymm1, ymm4
        vpsrlw  ymm3, ymm3, 4
        vpand   ymm3, ymm3, ymm0
        vpshufb ymm3, ymm2, ymm3
        vpor    ymm3, ymm4, ymm3
        vmovdqu ymmword ptr [rcx + rax], ymm3
        add     rax, 32
        cmp     rax, 1024
        jne     .LBB0_1
</code></pre>
<p>Here, <code>ymm0</code> is a nibble-mask <code>0x0F</code>, and <code>ymm1</code> and <code>ymm2</code> have been pre-initialized to lookup tables for the high and low nibbles before the loop.</p>
<p>Without <code>-fno-unroll-loops</code>, Clang loads four 128-bit vectors from memory on each iteration and ICX loads two. GCC 16.1 generates 32-bit shift and bitwise instructions, still roughly as good as the versions micro-optimized for 32-bit operations but missing the <code>rbit</code> optimization on ARM64. MSVC 19.50 for x64 doesn’t handle this well at all, using 8-bit operations, and MSVC for ARM64 does not even inline <code>revByte</code>, but generates <code>bl</code> instructions to it within the inner loop.</p>
<p>This is a good example of how, on good compilers, readable high-level code can optimize better than hacks designed for 20th-century hardware, but if the optimizer gets confused, it can be much worse than even a naive loop. You don’t want to get the compiler to generate 32-bit code for a target with 128-bit registers!</p>

Q: Don&#39;t see logs on the server side when using console.log A: <p><em><strong>Invoking <code>console.log(&quot;Hello World&quot;)</code> will log <code>&quot;Hello World&quot;</code> to the console where the code is running</strong></em>. If you are running this code using NodeJS, the logs will show up in the terminal where NodeJS is running. If on the other hand, this code is running in a browser, the logs will show up in the browser's console.</p>
<p>This particular code is running in the browser hence the log shows up in the browser's console. If you write the log on code that will run on the server, it will show up there.</p>
<p>OP's code:</p>
<pre><code>getUser.onclick = function () {
    console.log('Server running at http://127.0.0.1:1337/');
    var req = new XMLHttpRequest();
    req.onreadystatechange = function () {
      if (req.readyState === 4 && req.status === 200) {
        var user = JSON.parse(req.responseText);
        results.innerText += user.firstName + ' ' + user.lastName + '\n';
      }
    };
    req.open("GET", "/user");
    req.send();</code></pre>
